In article <25319@cs.yale.edu> beigel-richard@CS.YALE.EDU (Richard Beigel) writes:
>As for debts, I think that they do *owe* us a list of known serious
>bugs, posted here on a regular basis.  Instead I get complaints from
>them about how people keep emailing to them about "known" bugs.  When
>I used to maintain a game program, I felt sorry for people who got
>wiped out because of *my* programming mistakes.  Eventually I made
>sure that the game was recoverable after crashes.  Apparently such a
>feature has been implemented in nethack by a reader of this newsgroup;
>if it works, I think that the dev team "owes" it to us to distribute
>that in pl8.

     Now wait a minute.  I agree that these things would be nice, but I also
think that you have a lot of nerve coming around telling the development team
that they *owe* you something.
     Try the following simple exercise:
     Take the amount of money that you have paid for the game.
     Divide by the amount of time and anguish that it's taken the development
team to get the game to where it is now.
     The result (which I am betting is zero divided by a very large number) is
the exact amount that the development team *owes* you.

     If you have features that you want to see implemented in the game, you
have two options:
     1. Make the suggestion to the development team.  Wait until *they* decide
that the improvement to the game would be worth the time and aggravation of
implementing it.
     2. Program it yourself.  I'm sure the development team would welcome good
code.  Heck, maybe they'd make you a member, and you could find out exactly
how much fun fielding e-mail from appreciative users like yourself can be.

     Disclaimer: I have no connection with the development team, other than
doing some playtesting for pl8 (which should be out soon, and which contains
some quite nifty new features that I'm not going to tell you about :) ).
I do, however, work on a similar shared game project (a UW version of Moria,
which we'll hopefully release some day), and I think I have some idea of the
sort of things the NetHack development team goes through.  (Then again, maybe
not; we only have to support three machines.)
